// =======================================================
// caustic-av/video/vp9/decode.cst
// Frame driver for the VP9 decoder: a packet of 1..8 coded frame slices in,
// zero or more reconstructed 8-bit I420 output frames out. It validates the
// parsed header, selects a free pixel buffer from the caller's reference
// pool, binds the caller's work block, decodes the tiles through
// block.cst/tile.cst, runs the probability adaptation of section 6.1.2
// through adapt_bridge.cst, runs the loop filter of section 8.8, and performs
// the post-frame bookkeeping of sections 8.1, 8.9 and 8.10.
//
// Packet handoff
// --------------
// vp9_decoder_send takes one packet (a single frame or an Annex B
// superframe) and arms it. vp9_decoder_receive decodes slices until it has
// one frame to display and returns that frame; it returns 0 when the packet
// has no further output. A packet therefore yields 0..8 outputs, in bitstream
// order, without ever producing a placeholder frame. Hidden frames
// (show_frame == 0) update the reference slots without being returned, and
// show_existing_frame (section 8.9) returns a stored slot without decoding a
// tile. The caller must drain a packet (receive returns 0) before sending
// the next one; send refuses otherwise, so every frame of every packet is
// handed out exactly once.
//
// The packet bytes stay the caller's: send stores only their addresses and
// lengths, and receive decodes from them. They must not change or be released
// until receive has returned 0 for that packet. Decoding the packet lazily is
// what keeps the reference pool bound: at most one displayed frame exists
// outside the reference slots, so eight reference slots plus one decode
// target suffice (section 8.10's FrameStore is indexed by the same eight
// slots).
//
// Memory
// ------
// Nothing here allocates. The caller supplies:
//   * a persistent work block (vp9_decoder_bind). Its size for a maximum
//     visible width/height is vp9_work_size(width, height); it must stay the
//     same block for the decoder's lifetime because it also holds the
//     PrevSegmentIds map and the previous frame's motion field between
//     frames.
//   * up to nine pixel buffers (vp9_decoder_add_buffer / replace_buffer).
//     Eight are needed for the reference slots; the ninth guarantees that a
//     decode target that no published slot names always exists. Every buffer
//     is caller-owned and must stay alive while a reference slot names it;
//     vp9_refs_buffer_in_use answers when it is safe to reuse or free one.
//     With tile threads enabled, a fourth caller block holds the per-worker
//     contexts (vp9_decoder_bind_threads).
// Both are the caller's to free; vp9_decoder_free only drops views. The one
// allocation on the threaded path is inside std/thread.cst (a 2 MiB stack
// per spawned worker, unmapped by join).
//
// Threads
// -------
// Tile decoding can be spread over a caller-owned worker pool
// (threads.cst): vp9_decoder_bind_threads attaches a second block, sized
// with vp9_threads_work_size, and a worker count; vp9_decoder_set_threads
// changes the count afterwards. The default is the sequential path, and the
// two paths produce identical bytes for every valid stream. Per frame, one
// worker decodes one range of tile columns for all tile rows; the pool is
// spawned and joined inside that frame, and the probability adaptation, the
// loop filter, the segment/motion-field updates and publication stay on the
// calling thread after the join. A frame with one tile column uses the
// sequential walk. See threads.md for the context isolation, the memory
// layout and the error rules, including the I_NOMEM path that reports the
// required block size in d.thr.need.
//
// Reference lifetime and the decode target
// ----------------------------------------
// The decoder never reconstructs over a published reference: _decode_slice
// picks a pool buffer that is named by no slot (vp9_refs_buffer_in_use) and
// whose range is publishable (vp9_refs_buffer_publishable), and decodes
// there. After the loop filter the frame is published by vp9_refs_publish,
// which stores that view in every slot named by refresh_frame_flags, so the
// buffer becomes a reference in place (section 8.10's copy of CurrFrame into
// FrameStore, represented as a view because the pixels already are the
// frame). A frame with refresh_frame_flags 0 leaves the buffer unpublished;
// the next frame may reuse it once the caller has consumed the output view.
//
// Output lifetime
// ---------------
// The returned view points into a pool buffer. It is valid until the next
// vp9_decoder_receive or vp9_decoder_send call, whichever comes first; after
// that the buffer may be reused for another frame. If the frame was also
// published as a reference, its pixels stay valid while any slot names that
// buffer, so a show_existing view of it never expires as long as the slot is
// live. The decoder copies no pixels into a separate display surface.
//
// Padded surfaces
// ---------------
// Every pool buffer must hold frame.frame_size(coded_w, coded_h) bytes for
// coded_w = round64(MiCols*8) + 64 and the same for height. The extra
// 64-sample band on the right and bottom holds the overhang of edge blocks:
// 6.4.21 predicts whole transform/prediction blocks whose top-left is
// inside MiCols*8 / MiRows*8, and 8.5.1/8.5.2.4 write the whole block. The
// visible rectangle is (0,0)-(h.width,h.height); each selected buffer is
// zeroed before use so padding a filter touches has a defined value.
//
// What is decoded
// ---------------
// Key frames, intra-only frames, inter frames, compound prediction, scaled
// references, the eight reference slots, show_existing_frame, error-resilient
// frames and 1..64 x 1..4 tile layouts. Anything profile 0 cannot express
// (other profiles, 4:2:2/4:4:4, 10/12-bit, CS_RGB) returns I_UNSUPPORTED
// before a surface is touched, as does a stream whose probability or
// coefficient table blobs are still pending.
// =======================================================

use "../../core/errno.cst" as errno;
use "../../core/frame.cst" as frame;
use "vp9_tables.cst" as t;
use "header.cst" as hd;
use "residual.cst" as res;
use "residual_tables.cst" as res_tbl;
use "block.cst" as blk;
use "tile.cst" as tile;
use "loopfilter.cst" as lf;
use "refs.cst" as refs;
use "scale.cst" as scale;
use "inter.cst" as ip;
use "adapt.cst" as ad;
use "adapt_bridge.cst" as ab;
use "threads.cst" as threads;

// The mode-info grid ceiling: 65536 pixels per side / 8.
let is i64 as VP9_MAX_MI with imut = 8192;

// Pixel buffers the decoder can use: eight reference slots plus one
// transient decode target (at least one is always free; see above).
let is i64 as VP9_POOL_BUFFERS with imut = 9;

// Slices one superframe can carry (Annex B frame count field).
let is i64 as VP9_MAX_SUPERFRAME_FRAMES with imut = 8;

// One displayed frame. The view is I420, visible width/height, with the
// coded strides of the pool buffer; render_* is the 7.2.4 hint of the frame
// that filled the slot (for show_existing, the hint stored with the slot).
// order rises across every output of the stream and is only a hint for the
// caller's presentation queue. A frame is never returned twice: a
// show_existing frame is one output even though it reuses stored pixels.
struct Vp9Output {
    frame         as frame.Frame;
    render_width  as i64;
    render_height as i64;
    profile       as i64;
    bit_depth     as i64;
    subsampling_x as i64;
    subsampling_y as i64;
    color_space   as i64;
    color_range   as i64;
    order         as i64;
}

// ── caller-owned work block ─────────────────────────────────────────

// The per-frame arrays of block.cst and residual.cst, carved out of one
// caller-owned byte block. `need` is the total the layout requires; the
// pointers are valid only when the block was carved with a non-null base.
struct Vp9Work {
    need as i64;

    mi_cols as i64;
    mi_rows as i64;
    acols as i64;              // MiCols rounded up to 8 (partition ctx)
    arows as i64;
    nz_above_stride as i64;
    nz_left_stride as i64;

    mi_sizes as *u8;
    tx_sizes as *u8;
    skips as *u8;
    segment_ids as *u8;
    ref_frames as *u8;         // two per mode-info block
    y_modes as *u8;
    sub_modes as *u8;
    prev_seg_ids as *u8;
    interp_filters as *u8;
    prev_ref_frames as *u8;    // two per mode-info block

    above_part as *u8;
    left_part as *u8;
    above_segpred as *u8;
    left_segpred as *u8;
    above_nz as *u8;
    left_nz as *u8;

    mvs as *i32;               // [mi][2][2]
    sub_mvs as *i32;           // [mi][2][4][2]
    prev_mvs as *i32;          // [mi][2][2]

    coef as *i64;
    scratch as *u8;            // inter prediction intermediate samples
    cc as res.Vp9CoefCtx;
}

fn _zero(p as *u8, n as i64) as void {
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < n) { p[i] = cast(u8, 0); i = i + 1; }
}

// Round a non-negative offset up to 8 bytes.
fn _align8(off as i64) as i64 {
    return (off + 7) & (0 - 8);
}

// Pointer at `off` bytes into the caller's base block. A null base yields a
// null/absolute pointer, which is never dereferenced (only the layout path
// of vp9_work_size carves with a null base).
fn _at(base as *u8, off as i64) as *u8 {
    return cast(*u8, cast(i64, base) + off);
}

// Lay out (and, when base is non-null, bind) the work arrays for a frame of
// mi_cols x mi_rows mode-info units. Returns 0 on success; I_NOMEM with
// w.need set when the caller's block is too small; I_DIMS/I_BADPARAM for
// impossible dimensions or a misaligned base. Dimensions are capped at
// VP9_MAX_MI before any size is computed, so every sum below fits in i64.
fn _work_carve(w as *Vp9Work, base as *u8, cap as i64,
               mi_cols as i64, mi_rows as i64,
               ss_x as i64, ss_y as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, w) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (mi_cols <= 0 || mi_rows <= 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (mi_cols > VP9_MAX_MI || mi_rows > VP9_MAX_MI) { return errno.I_DIMS; }

    let is i64 as mi_count = mi_cols * mi_rows;
    let is i64 as sb64_cols = (mi_cols + 7) >> 3;
    let is i64 as sb64_rows = (mi_rows + 7) >> 3;
    let is i64 as acols = sb64_cols << 3;
    let is i64 as arows = sb64_rows << 3;
    if (acols < mi_cols || arows < mi_rows) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    let is i64 as nz_above = 2 * acols + 16;
    let is i64 as nz_left = 2 * arows + 16;

    let is i64 as off with mut = 0;

    w.mi_sizes = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.tx_sizes = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.skips = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.segment_ids = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.ref_frames = _at(base, off); off = off + 2 * mi_count;
    w.y_modes = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.sub_modes = _at(base, off); off = off + 4 * mi_count;
    w.prev_seg_ids = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.interp_filters = _at(base, off); off = off + mi_count;
    w.prev_ref_frames = _at(base, off); off = off + 2 * mi_count;
    w.above_part = _at(base, off); off = off + acols;
    w.left_part = _at(base, off); off = off + arows;
    w.above_segpred = _at(base, off); off = off + acols;
    w.left_segpred = _at(base, off); off = off + arows;
    w.above_nz = _at(base, off); off = off + 3 * nz_above;
    w.left_nz = _at(base, off); off = off + 3 * nz_left;

    off = _align8(off);
    w.mvs = cast(*i32, _at(base, off)); off = off + 4 * mi_count * 4;
    w.sub_mvs = cast(*i32, _at(base, off)); off = off + 16 * mi_count * 4;
    w.prev_mvs = cast(*i32, _at(base, off)); off = off + 4 * mi_count * 4;

    off = _align8(off);
    w.coef = cast(*i64, _at(base, off));
    off = off + 1024 * 8;

    w.scratch = _at(base, off); off = off + ip.INTER_SCRATCH_BYTES;

    w.need = off;
    w.mi_cols = mi_cols;
    w.mi_rows = mi_rows;
    w.acols = acols;
    w.arows = arows;
    w.nz_above_stride = nz_above;
    w.nz_left_stride = nz_left;

    // Every pointer above is plain base+offset arithmetic (`_at`); none is
    // dereferenced until the null-base, alignment and capacity checks have
    // passed. `w.need` is the layout total queried by vp9_work_size.
    if (cast(i64, base) == 0) { return errno.I_OK; }
    if ((cast(i64, base) & 7) != 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (cap < off) { return errno.I_NOMEM; }

    let is i64 as st = res.vp9_coef_ctx_init(&w.cc, w.above_nz, nz_above,
                                             w.left_nz, nz_left,
                                             mi_cols, mi_rows, ss_x, ss_y);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Bytes vp9_decoder_bind needs for a decoder that will see frames up to
// width x height. Returns a positive byte count, or I_DIMS for an impossible
// size. This is the visible size: the coded padding is not part of the work
// block.
fn vp9_work_size(width as i64, height as i64) as i64 {
    if (width <= 0 || height <= 0) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (width > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM || height > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM) {
        return errno.I_DIMS;
    }
    let is i64 as mi_cols = (width + 7) >> 3;
    let is i64 as mi_rows = (height + 7) >> 3;
    let is Vp9Work as w;
    let is i64 as st = _work_carve(&w, cast(*u8, 0), 0, mi_cols, mi_rows, 1, 1);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    return w.need;
}

// Bytes vp9_decoder_bind_threads needs for a decoder that will see frames up
// to width x height with `workers` threads decoding in parallel (the calling
// thread included). Like vp9_work_size this is the visible size: each worker
// needs its private left contexts, coefficient buffer, inter-prediction
// scratch and counter set, sized from the frame's mode-info grid. `workers`
// is clamped to the pool's slot count, and a value below 2 gives the size
// the sequential decoder needs, i.e. zero.
//
// The block is separate from the work block on purpose: the work block is
// sized exactly by vp9_work_size and callers already allocate it, while a
// decoder that never enables threads should not pay for the worker slots.
fn vp9_threads_work_size(width as i64, height as i64,
                         workers as i64) as i64 {
    if (width <= 0 || height <= 0) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (width > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM || height > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM) {
        return errno.I_DIMS;
    }
    if (workers < 1) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    let is i64 as mi_cols = (width + 7) >> 3;
    let is i64 as mi_rows = (height + 7) >> 3;
    if (mi_cols <= 0 || mi_rows <= 0) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (mi_cols > VP9_MAX_MI || mi_rows > VP9_MAX_MI) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (workers < 2) { return 0; }
    return threads.vp9_threads_size(mi_cols, mi_rows, workers);
}

// Coded grid for a visible size: MiCols*8 x MiRows*8 rounded up to 64, plus
// one 64-sample band for the overhanging blocks of 6.4.21/8.5.1.
fn _coded_dims(width as i64, height as i64,
               out_w as *i64, out_h as *i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, out_w) == 0 || cast(i64, out_h) == 0) {
        return errno.I_BADPARAM;
    }
    if (width <= 0 || height <= 0) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (width > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM || height > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM) {
        return errno.I_DIMS;
    }
    let is i64 as mi_cols = (width + 7) >> 3;
    let is i64 as mi_rows = (height + 7) >> 3;
    if (mi_cols <= 0 || mi_rows <= 0) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    if (mi_cols > VP9_MAX_MI || mi_rows > VP9_MAX_MI) { return errno.I_DIMS; }
    let is i64 as maxx = mi_cols * 8;
    let is i64 as maxy = mi_rows * 8;
    let is i64 as coded_w = (((maxx + 63) >> 6) << 6) + 64;
    let is i64 as coded_h = (((maxy + 63) >> 6) << 6) + 64;
    if (coded_w > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM || coded_h > frame.FRAME_MAX_DIM) {
        return errno.I_DIMS;
    }
    *out_w = coded_w;
    *out_h = coded_h;
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Bytes one pool buffer needs for a visible width x height frame, i.e. the
// value vp9_decoder_receive reports in frame_need. Returns a positive byte
// count or a negative code.
fn vp9_frame_size(width as i64, height as i64) as i64 {
    let is i64 as cw with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as ch with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as st = _coded_dims(width, height, &cw, &ch);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    return frame.frame_size(cw, ch);
}

// ── decoder object ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

struct Vp9Decoder {
    state as t.Vp9State;
    hdr as hd.Vp9Header;
    refs as refs.Vp9Refs;

    work as *u8;
    work_cap as i64;
    work_need as i64;      // set when receive finds the work too small
    frame_need as i64;     // set when receive finds no usable pixel buffer

    pool_mem as [9]*u8;    // caller-owned pixel buffers
    pool_cap as [9]i64;
    pool_count as i64;
    frame_serial as i64;   // order hint of the next decoded coded frame
    out_serial as i64;     // order hint of the next returned output

    // Optional tile-threaded decode (threads.cst). Zero is the sequential
    // path: no worker block bound, no workers requested. The pool holds the
    // thread handles; the caller's block holds the per-worker contexts.
    thr as threads.Vp9Threads;

    // The packet armed by vp9_decoder_send and consumed by
    // vp9_decoder_receive. The pointers belong to the caller and stay valid
    // until the packet is drained (receive returns 0).
    pkt as hd.Vp9Superframe;
    pkt_index as i64;      // next slice to decode, 0..pkt.count
    pkt_active as i64;     // 1 between send and the final receive

    // adapt_bridge.cst scratch: one working probability set, the counts of
    // this frame, one import slot for a saved context and the §9.3 trees.
    ap as ad.Vp9Probs;
    acnt as ad.Vp9Counts;
    apctx as ad.Vp9ProbContexts;
    atrees as ad.Vp9Trees;
}

// Zero the decoder and initialise the persistent state. No frame context is
// valid yet; a key or intra-only frame installs one (section 7.2).
fn vp9_decoder_init(d as *Vp9Decoder) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    _zero(cast(*u8, d), sizeof(Vp9Decoder));
    t.vp9_state_init(&d.state);
    refs.vp9_refs_init(&d.refs);
    threads.vp9_threads_init(&d.thr);
    d.pool_count = 0;
    d.frame_serial = 0;
    return ab.vp9_adapt_trees_init(&d.atrees);
}

// Attach the caller's persistent work block. `cap` is its size in bytes; the
// exact requirement is checked per frame against vp9_work_size. The block
// must stay alive and 8-byte aligned for the decoder's lifetime.
fn vp9_decoder_bind(d as *Vp9Decoder, work as *u8, cap as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (cast(i64, work) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (cap <= 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    d.work = work;
    d.work_cap = cap;
    d.work_need = 0;
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Attach the caller's worker block and enable tile threads. `workers` counts
// the calling thread, so 2 means one spawned worker; 0 or 1 disables the
// threaded path and needs no block. Values above the pool's slot count are
// clamped. The block must stay alive and 8-byte aligned while threads are
// enabled and is sized by vp9_threads_work_size for the largest frame the
// decoder will see; each frame re-checks the capacity and receive reports
// I_NOMEM (with d.thr.need set) if the block was too small for that frame.
//
// Disabling with vp9_decoder_bind_threads(d, 0, 0, 0) or
// vp9_decoder_set_threads(d, 1) restores the sequential path; output is
// identical in both modes. Call this between packets, never while a packet
// is being drained.
fn vp9_decoder_bind_threads(d as *Vp9Decoder, mem as *u8, cap as i64,
                            workers as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    return threads.vp9_threads_bind(&d.thr, mem, cap, workers);
}

// Change the worker count after vp9_decoder_bind_threads. Returns the
// effective count (the calling thread included), 0 or 1 meaning sequential,
// or I_BADPARAM when more than one worker is requested while no block is
// bound.
fn vp9_decoder_set_threads(d as *Vp9Decoder, workers as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    return threads.vp9_threads_set_workers(&d.thr, workers);
}

// A pixel range is usable when it is non-null, non-negative, bounded by the
// frame ceiling, and cannot wrap when its end is computed.
fn _range_ok(mem as *u8, cap as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, mem) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (cap <= 0 || cap > frame.FRAME_MAX_BYTES) { return 0; }
    let is i64 as a = cast(i64, mem);
    if (a <= 0 || a > 9223372036854775807 - cap) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

// 1 when [mem, mem+cap) overlaps a registered pool buffer other than skip.
fn _pool_overlaps(d as *Vp9Decoder, mem as *u8, cap as i64, skip as i64) as i64 {
    let is i64 as a0 = cast(i64, mem);
    let is i64 as a1 = a0 + cap;
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < d.pool_count) {
        if (i != skip) {
            let is i64 as s0 = cast(i64, d.pool_mem[i]);
            let is i64 as s1 = s0 + d.pool_cap[i];
            if (a0 < s1 && s0 < a1) { return 1; }
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Register one caller-owned pixel buffer. Buffers must not overlap each
// other: two IDs naming the same memory would let a decode target alias a
// published reference. Up to VP9_POOL_BUFFERS are accepted.
fn vp9_decoder_add_buffer(d as *Vp9Decoder, mem as *u8, cap as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (_range_ok(mem, cap) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (d.pool_count < 0 || d.pool_count >= VP9_POOL_BUFFERS) {
        return errno.I_NOMEM;
    }
    if (_pool_overlaps(d, mem, cap, 0 - 1) == 1) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    let is i64 as i = d.pool_count;
    d.pool_mem[i] = mem;
    d.pool_cap[i] = cap;
    d.pool_count = i + 1;
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Index of a registered buffer that no reference slot names, or -1 when the
// pool is empty. At most eight slots can name buffers, so with nine
// registered at least one index is free; this is the index a caller passes to
// vp9_decoder_replace_buffer after I_NOMEM.
fn vp9_decoder_free_buffer(d as *Vp9Decoder) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < d.pool_count) {
        if (refs.vp9_refs_buffer_in_use(&d.refs, i) == 0) { return i; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Replace one registered buffer, for a caller that must grow a free buffer
// after a resolution increase. A buffer still named by a reference slot
// cannot be replaced: its pixels are live.
fn vp9_decoder_replace_buffer(d as *Vp9Decoder, idx as i64,
                              mem as *u8, cap as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (idx < 0 || idx >= d.pool_count) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (refs.vp9_refs_buffer_in_use(&d.refs, idx) == 1) {
        return errno.I_BADPARAM;
    }
    if (_range_ok(mem, cap) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (_pool_overlaps(d, mem, cap, idx) == 1) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    d.pool_mem[idx] = mem;
    d.pool_cap[idx] = cap;
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Drop every reference and make all buffers free again, for a resync that
// discards the stream state. The caller's memory is untouched.
fn vp9_decoder_reset_refs(d as *Vp9Decoder) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    return refs.vp9_refs_reset(&d.state, &d.refs);
}

// Drop the views; the caller keeps ownership of the work and pixel blocks.
// Reference slots are invalidated too, so a decoder cannot be used again
// with stale slot metadata.
fn vp9_decoder_free(d as *Vp9Decoder) as void {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return; }
    refs.vp9_refs_reset(&d.state, &d.refs);
    threads.vp9_threads_init(&d.thr);
    d.work = cast(*u8, 0);
    d.work_cap = 0;
    d.work_need = 0;
    d.frame_need = 0;
    d.pool_count = 0;
    d.pkt_active = 0;
    d.pkt_index = 0;
    d.pkt.count = 0;
    d.pkt.index_size = 0;
}

// Index of a buffer that can host `need` bytes, is not named by a published
// reference slot, and whose range is publishable, or -1. The publishable
// check repeats the id lookup against the actual pixel ranges, so a caller
// that mixed two bookkeeping schemes still cannot reconstruct over a live
// reference.
fn _pick_buffer(d as *Vp9Decoder, need as i64) as i64 {
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < d.pool_count) {
        if (d.pool_cap[i] >= need
            && refs.vp9_refs_buffer_in_use(&d.refs, i) == 0
            && refs.vp9_refs_buffer_publishable(&d.refs, d.pool_mem[i],
                                                d.pool_cap[i]) == 1) {
            return i;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return 0 - 1;
}

// Copy a frame view field by field (the memory is not copied).
fn _frame_copy(d as *frame.Frame, s as *frame.Frame) as void {
    d.width = s.width;
    d.height = s.height;
    d.coded_width = s.coded_width;
    d.coded_height = s.coded_height;
    d.y = s.y;
    d.u = s.u;
    d.v = s.v;
    d.stride_y = s.stride_y;
    d.stride_u = s.stride_u;
    d.stride_v = s.stride_v;
    d.pixels = s.pixels;
    d.cap = s.cap;
}

// ── frame-level loop filter ─────────────────────────────────────────

fn _loop_filter(d as *Vp9Decoder, b as *blk.Vp9Blk, out as *frame.Frame) as i64 {
    let is *hd.Vp9Header as h = b.h;
    if (h.lf_level == 0) { return errno.I_OK; }

    let is [8]u8 as alt_active;
    let is [8]i64 as alt_data;
    let is i64 as s with mut = 0;
    while (s < 8) {
        alt_active[s] = cast(u8, h.seg_feature_enabled[s][t.VP9_SEG_LVL_ALT_L]);
        alt_data[s] = h.seg_feature_data[s][t.VP9_SEG_LVL_ALT_L];
        s = s + 1;
    }

    let is lf.LfParams as lp;
    lp.mi_cols = b.mi_cols;
    lp.mi_rows = b.mi_rows;
    lp.sub_x = b.sub_x;
    lp.sub_y = b.sub_y;
    lp.level = h.lf_level;
    lp.sharpness = h.lf_sharpness;
    lp.delta_enabled = h.lf_delta_enabled;
    lp.seg_enabled = h.seg_enabled;
    lp.seg_abs_or_delta = h.seg_abs_or_delta;
    lp.ref_deltas = &h.lf_ref_delta[0];
    lp.mode_deltas = &h.lf_mode_delta[0];
    lp.seg_alt_l_active = &alt_active[0];
    lp.seg_alt_l_data = &alt_data[0];

    let is lf.LfFrame as lfr;
    let is i64 as st = lf.lf_frame_init(&lfr, &lp);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }

    let is lf.LfBlockInfo as lbi;
    lbi.mi_sizes = b.mi_sizes;
    lbi.tx_sizes = b.tx_sizes;
    lbi.skips = b.skips;
    lbi.segment_ids = b.segment_ids;
    lbi.ref_frames = b.ref_frames;
    lbi.ref_stride = 2;
    lbi.y_modes = b.y_modes;

    return lf.lf_filter_frame(&lfr, &lbi, out.y, out.u, out.v,
                              out.stride_y, out.stride_u, out.stride_v);
}

// ── post-frame bookkeeping ──────────────────────────────────────────

// Section 8.1 step 3: after a frame whose segmentation updates the map,
// PrevSegmentIds follows SegmentIds. The clears that sections 7.2 and 7.2.6
// require happen before the tiles (see _decode_slice), so a block that
// predicts its segment id through get_segment_id (6.4.14) reads the
// retained previous map on a normal inter frame, or the cleared map on an
// intra/error-resilient frame or after a size change.
fn _update_seg_map(w as *Vp9Work, b as *blk.Vp9Blk, h as *hd.Vp9Header) as void {
    if (h.seg_enabled != 1 || h.seg_update_map != 1) { return; }
    let is i64 as n = b.mi_cols * b.mi_rows;
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        w.prev_seg_ids[i] = b.segment_ids[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// Section 8.10 step 2: the motion field of this frame becomes PrevMvs and
// PrevRefFrames for the next same-size frame. use_prev_frame_mvs is only 1
// when the size matches, so the layout of the two arrays is the same when
// they are next read.
fn _update_prev_mv(w as *Vp9Work, b as *blk.Vp9Blk) as void {
    let is i64 as n = b.mi_cols * b.mi_rows;
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < 2 * n) {
        w.prev_ref_frames[i] = b.ref_frames[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    i = 0;
    while (i < 4 * n) {
        w.prev_mvs[i] = b.mvs[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

// ── one coded frame slice ───────────────────────────────────────────

// Fill one output record from a published slot view. Used by the
// show_existing path, which takes every geometry and format field from the
// slot and never from the current header (7.2.6's note: neither frame_size
// nor compute_image_size ran for it).
fn _out_from_slot(out as *Vp9Output, v as *refs.Vp9RefView, order as i64) as void {
    _frame_copy(&out.frame, &v.view);
    out.render_width = v.render_width;
    out.render_height = v.render_height;
    out.profile = v.profile;
    out.bit_depth = v.bit_depth;
    out.subsampling_x = v.subsampling_x;
    out.subsampling_y = v.subsampling_y;
    out.color_space = v.color_space;
    out.color_range = v.color_range;
    out.order = order;
}

// Decode one coded frame slice, in bitstream order. Returns 1 when the slice
// produced a displayed frame (written to out), 0 when it was hidden
// (show_frame == 0) or produced no output, or a negative code. A packet is
// consumed one slice at a time so a displayed frame never forces a second
// output buffer to exist beside the eight reference slots.
fn _decode_slice(d as *Vp9Decoder, data as *u8, len as i64,
                 out as *Vp9Output) as i64 {
    let is i64 as st with mut = hd.vp9_header_parse(&d.state, &d.hdr, data, len);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    let is *hd.Vp9Header as h = &d.hdr;

    // Section 8.9: output a stored reference. Nothing is decoded, no tile is
    // read, no slot or persistent field changes (refresh_frame_flags is 0 and
    // 8.10 step 2 is skipped when show_existing_frame is 1). The header
    // already checked that Vp9State believes the slot is published; this
    // check is against the pixel slots themselves.
    if (h.show_existing_frame == 1) {
        let is refs.Vp9RefView as v;
        st = refs.vp9_refs_show_existing(&d.refs, h.frame_to_show_map_idx, &v);
        if (st < 0) { return st; }
        _out_from_slot(out, &v, d.out_serial);
        d.out_serial = d.out_serial + 1;
        return 1;
    }
    // The probability blobs and the coefficient tables must exist before any
    // tile is decoded; both modules report their generated state.
    if (h.probs_pending == 1 || t.vp9_tables_ready() == 0) {
        return errno.I_UNSUPPORTED;
    }
    if (res_tbl.vp9_residual_tables_ready() == 0) {
        return errno.I_UNSUPPORTED;
    }
    // Profile 0 is fixed to 8-bit 4:2:0 by header.cst; restate the surface
    // requirement before touching memory.
    if (h.bit_depth != 8 || h.subsampling_x != 1 || h.subsampling_y != 1) {
        return errno.I_UNSUPPORTED;
    }
    if (h.has_compressed_header != 1) { return errno.I_CORRUPT; }
    if (h.mi_cols <= 0 || h.mi_rows <= 0) { return errno.I_CORRUPT; }
    if (h.mi_cols > VP9_MAX_MI || h.mi_rows > VP9_MAX_MI) {
        return errno.I_DIMS;
    }

    // Every reference an inter frame selects must be published, match this
    // frame's format, and at least one must satisfy the section 7.2.5 size
    // ratio. The header checked the format against Vp9State; this repeats it
    // against the pixel slots, then applies the ratio bound scale.cst owns.
    if (h.frame_is_intra == 0) {
        st = refs.vp9_refs_check_header(&d.refs, h);
        if (st < 0) { return st; }
        let is i64 as ok with mut = 0;
        let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
        while (i < 3) {
            let is refs.Vp9RefView as v;
            st = refs.vp9_refs_get(&d.refs, h.ref_frame_idx[i], &v);
            if (st < 0) { return st; }
            if (scale.vp9_scale_ratio_ok(h.width, h.height,
                                         v.view.width, v.view.height) == 1) {
                ok = 1;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (ok == 0) { return errno.I_CORRUPT; }
    }

    // Coded surface: MiCols*8 x MiRows*8 rounded to 64, plus one 64-sample
    // band for the overhanging blocks of 6.4.21/8.5.1.
    let is i64 as coded_w with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as coded_h with mut = 0;
    st = _coded_dims(h.width, h.height, &coded_w, &coded_h);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    let is i64 as fsz = frame.frame_size(coded_w, coded_h);
    if (fsz < 0) { return fsz; }

    let is i64 as buf = _pick_buffer(d, fsz);
    if (buf < 0) { d.frame_need = fsz; return errno.I_NOMEM; }
    let is *u8 as mem = d.pool_mem[buf];
    let is i64 as cap = d.pool_cap[buf];

    let is frame.Frame as cf;
    st = frame.frame_alloc_coded(&cf, h.width, h.height, coded_w, coded_h,
                                 mem, cap);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    // Padding samples are not reconstructed by every block; zeroing the
    // coded surface makes whatever the loop filter touches deterministic.
    _zero(mem, fsz);

    let is Vp9Work as w;
    st = _work_carve(&w, d.work, d.work_cap, h.mi_cols, h.mi_rows,
                     h.subsampling_x, h.subsampling_y);
    if (st < 0) {
        if (st == errno.I_NOMEM) { d.work_need = w.need; }
        return st;
    }

    // Mvs and SubMvs of an intra block are never assigned (6.4.4), and
    // 6.5.10/6.5.8 read both lists of a candidate regardless of its
    // reference frames; the specification's arrays start each frame
    // cleared. PrevMvs later copies this all-zero field for intra blocks.
    _zero(cast(*u8, w.mvs), 4 * (h.mi_cols * h.mi_rows) * 4);
    _zero(cast(*u8, w.sub_mvs), 16 * (h.mi_cols * h.mi_rows) * 4);

    // SubModes is written only for an intra block (6.4.4); the neighbour
    // mode read of 9.3.2 must see DC_PRED for an inter block, as libvpx
    // does (`vp9_above_block_mode`/`vp9_left_block_mode` return DC_PRED for
    // an inter neighbour). MODE_DC is zero, so a per-frame clear gives an
    // unwritten cell exactly that default.
    _zero(w.sub_modes, 4 * (h.mi_cols * h.mi_rows));

    let is blk.Vp9Blk as b;
    b.h = h;
    b.st = &d.state;
    b.fr = &cf;
    b.mi_cols = h.mi_cols;
    b.mi_rows = h.mi_rows;
    b.mi_col_start = 0;
    b.mi_col_end = h.mi_cols;
    b.acols = w.acols;
    b.arows = w.arows;
    b.sub_x = h.subsampling_x;
    b.sub_y = h.subsampling_y;
    b.lossless = h.lossless;
    b.mi_sizes = w.mi_sizes;
    b.tx_sizes = w.tx_sizes;
    b.skips = w.skips;
    b.segment_ids = w.segment_ids;
    b.ref_frames = w.ref_frames;
    b.y_modes = w.y_modes;
    b.sub_modes = w.sub_modes;
    b.prev_seg_ids = w.prev_seg_ids;
    b.interp_filters = w.interp_filters;
    b.mvs = w.mvs;
    b.sub_mvs = w.sub_mvs;
    b.prev_ref_frames = w.prev_ref_frames;
    b.prev_mvs = w.prev_mvs;
    b.above_part = w.above_part;
    b.left_part = w.left_part;
    b.above_segpred = w.above_segpred;
    b.left_segpred = w.left_segpred;
    b.above_nz = w.above_nz;
    b.left_nz = w.left_nz;
    b.nz_above_stride = w.nz_above_stride;
    b.nz_left_stride = w.nz_left_stride;
    b.refs = &d.refs;
    b.scratch = w.scratch;
    b.cc = &w.cc;
    b.coef = w.coef;
    // Sequential path: count into Vp9State.counts. The threaded path
    // replaces this per worker in threads.cst.
    b.counts = cast(*t.Vp9Counts, 0);

    // Sections 7.2 and 7.2.6, before any block can read PrevSegmentIds
    // through get_segment_id (6.4.14): setup_past_independence zeroes the
    // whole map for every intra or error-resilient frame, and
    // compute_image_size does the same when the frame size is first seen or
    // changes. The header cannot perform the first clear because the work
    // block is bound per frame. Only a successful frame then copies its
    // SegmentIds over the map (8.1 step 3, _update_seg_map).
    if (h.frame_is_intra == 1 || h.error_resilient_mode == 1
        || h.seg_map_clear == 1) {
        _zero(w.prev_seg_ids, h.mi_cols * h.mi_rows);
    }

    st = tile.tile_decode_all(&b, data, len, &d.thr);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }

    // Section 6.1.2 refresh_probs, through the adapt.cst layout.
    st = ab.vp9_adapt_frame(&d.state, &d.ap, &d.acnt, &d.apctx, &d.atrees,
                            h.frame_context_idx, h.error_resilient_mode,
                            h.frame_parallel_decoding_mode, h.frame_is_intra,
                            h.refresh_frame_context, h.last_frame_type,
                            h.interpolation_filter, h.tx_mode,
                            h.allow_high_precision_mv);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }

    st = _loop_filter(d, &b, &cf);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }

    _update_seg_map(&w, &b, h);
    _update_prev_mv(&w, &b);

    // Section 8.10 step 1, represented as a view of the transient buffer
    // that no slot named before this frame.
    let is refs.Vp9RefFrame as rf;
    rf.valid = 1;
    rf.key_frame = 0;
    if (h.frame_type == t.VP9_KEY_FRAME) { rf.key_frame = 1; }
    rf.refresh_flags = h.refresh_frame_flags;
    rf.profile = h.profile;
    rf.bit_depth = h.bit_depth;
    rf.subsampling_x = h.subsampling_x;
    rf.subsampling_y = h.subsampling_y;
    rf.color_space = h.color_space;
    rf.color_range = h.color_range;
    rf.render_width = h.render_width;
    rf.render_height = h.render_height;
    rf.id = buf;
    rf.order = d.frame_serial;
    _frame_copy(&rf.view, &cf);
    st = refs.vp9_refs_publish(&d.state, &d.refs, &rf);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    d.frame_serial = d.frame_serial + 1;

    // Section 8.9's second branch: show_frame == 1 displays the frame just
    // decoded. A hidden frame only updated the reference state above.
    if (h.show_frame == 0) { return 0; }
    _frame_copy(&out.frame, &cf);
    out.render_width = h.render_width;
    out.render_height = h.render_height;
    out.profile = h.profile;
    out.bit_depth = h.bit_depth;
    out.subsampling_x = h.subsampling_x;
    out.subsampling_y = h.subsampling_y;
    out.color_space = h.color_space;
    out.color_range = h.color_range;
    out.order = d.out_serial;
    d.out_serial = d.out_serial + 1;
    return 1;
}

// ── public entry points ─────────────────────────────────────────────

// Arm one packet for vp9_decoder_receive. A packet is either a single coded
// frame or an Annex B superframe of 1..8 frames; the split is validated here
// (sizes, markers, ranges) so receive only walks slices already known to lie
// inside the caller's buffer. The packet bytes are not copied: data must stay
// valid and unchanged until receive has returned 0 for this packet.
//
// The previous packet must be drained first. Refusing send otherwise is what
// guarantees that no output of a packet can be skipped or handed out twice.
//
// Errors: I_BADPARAM for a null decoder, an unbound decoder, a null or
// negative buffer, a bad length, or a packet still being consumed; I_EOF for
// an empty packet; I_CORRUPT for a malformed superframe index; I_DIMS for
// more than VP9_MAX_SUPERFRAME_FRAMES frames.
fn vp9_decoder_send(d as *Vp9Decoder, data as *u8, len as i64) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (cast(i64, d.work) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (d.pkt_active != 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (len < 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (len == 0) { return errno.I_EOF; }
    if (cast(i64, data) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    d.frame_need = 0;
    d.work_need = 0;
    d.thr.need = 0;

    let is hd.Vp9Superframe as sf;
    let is i64 as st = hd.vp9_superframe_split(data, len, &sf);
    if (st < 0) { return st; }
    if (sf.count < 1) { return errno.I_CORRUPT; }
    if (sf.count > VP9_MAX_SUPERFRAME_FRAMES) { return errno.I_DIMS; }

    d.pkt.count = sf.count;
    d.pkt.index_size = sf.index_size;
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    while (i < sf.count) {
        d.pkt.ptr[i] = sf.ptr[i];
        d.pkt.len[i] = sf.len[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    d.pkt_index = 0;
    d.pkt_active = 1;
    return errno.I_OK;
}

// Hand out the next displayed frame of the armed packet. Returns 1 when out
// holds a frame, 0 when the packet produced no further output (the packet is
// then finished and a new send is allowed), or a negative code. Hidden frames
// and show-existing frames are handled in bitstream order:
//
//   * a hidden coded frame updates the reference slots and yields no output;
//   * a shown coded frame yields its freshly reconstructed pixels;
//   * show_existing_frame yields the stored slot named by
//     frame_to_show_map_idx without decoding a tile.
//
// out is cleared before decoding. Its view is valid until the next receive or
// send call; see the output-lifetime note at the top. No placeholder frame is
// ever produced: a packet whose slices are all hidden returns 0.
//
// Errors: I_BADPARAM for null pointers or an unbound decoder, I_NOMEM when
// the bound work block is too small (d.work_need), the bound worker block is
// too small for this frame's worker slots (d.thr.need, when tile threads are
// enabled), or no free pixel buffer can host the next frame (d.frame_need,
// then vp9_decoder_replace_buffer on a free index), I_UNSUPPORTED for
// pending table blobs or a profile-0 surface this library does not produce,
// and the header/tile modules' negative codes otherwise. An error abandons
// the rest of the packet; the persistent state is tainted until the next key
// or intra-only frame, as recorded in vp9.md. A tile-threaded failure joins
// every worker before returning, so no thread outlives the call.
fn vp9_decoder_receive(d as *Vp9Decoder, out as *Vp9Output) as i64 {
    if (cast(i64, d) == 0 || cast(i64, out) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    if (cast(i64, d.work) == 0) { return errno.I_BADPARAM; }
    frame.frame_free(&out.frame);
    out.render_width = 0;
    out.render_height = 0;
    out.profile = 0;
    out.bit_depth = 0;
    out.subsampling_x = 0;
    out.subsampling_y = 0;
    out.color_space = 0;
    out.color_range = 0;
    out.order = 0 - 1;

    while (d.pkt_active == 1 && d.pkt_index < d.pkt.count) {
        let is i64 as i = d.pkt_index;
        let is i64 as st = _decode_slice(d, d.pkt.ptr[i], d.pkt.len[i], out);
        d.pkt_index = i + 1;
        if (st < 0) {
            d.pkt_active = 0;
            frame.frame_free(&out.frame);
            return st;
        }
        if (st == 1) { return 1; }
    }
    d.pkt_active = 0;
    return 0;
}
